@cjihrig I'd like to pick this up and started poking around in the places you mentioned. If anyone else has more context here that would also be helpful as I can use all the help I can get.

As you suggested, I first dug into the test_runner/utils `parseCommandLine` method but I'm already seeing it would be somewhat complicated to additionally parse out supported node options (at least test options) from argv without changing the internal option parser (at least at first glance).

eg, running: 
```bash
./node --test --test-name-pattern='^.' asdf/test.js --test-only
```

the [`options_parser::Parse`](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/src/node.cc#L733) reports:
```
args: ./node asdf/test.js --test-only
exec_args: --test --test-name-pattern=^.
```
so it splits out the exec_args but stops as soon as it hits the positional arg, as expected, and `parseCommandLine` then receieves:
```
argv: ["<cwd>/node", "asdf/test.js", "--test-only"]
execArgv: ["--test", "--test-name-pattern=^."]
```

In this case, in order to additionally process `argv`, it seems like we would need to re-process it since we can't simply call `getOptionValue` for the remaining test options (`getOptionValue('--test-only')=false)`.

It seems to me like we would need to manually check (and maintain) all supported node options in the argv array in order to do this (eg using `util.parseArgs `we would still need to define all the options / params).

My first feeling would be something like this, but it seems very janky and a maintenance nightmare:
```js
function parseArgv() {
  const argvOptions = util.parseArgs({
    options: {
      'test': { type: 'boolean' },
      'test-force-exit': { type: 'boolean' },
      'test-only': { type: 'boolean' },
      // ...
    },
    allowPositionals: true,
    strict: true,
  });

  // eg
  return [values, positionals];
}

function getOptionValueOrArgv(flag, argvOptions) {
  const argvOption = argvOptions[flag.replace(/^--/, '')];

  if (typeof argvOption !== 'undefined') {
    return argvOption;
  }

  return getOptionValue(flag);
}

function parseCommandLine() {
  if (globalTestOptions) {
    return globalTestOptions;
  }

  const [argvOptions, positionals] = parseArgv();

  const isTestRunner = getOptionValueOrArgv('--test', argvOptions);
  const coverage = getOptionValueOrArgv('--experimental-test-coverage', argvOptions);
  const forceExit = getOptionValueOrArgv('--test-force-exit', argvOptions);
  const sourceMaps = getOptionValueOrArgv('--enable-source-maps', argvOptions);
  const updateSnapshots = getOptionValueOrArgv('--test-update-snapshots', argvOptions);
```

I would be simpler if the internal arg parser handled all internal flags, even after positionals, but I'm guessing this would break just about everything. Possibly changing the parser to push positionals to the end when --test is true would be easier, but of course that would be inconsistent.
What I would like is to call the internal option parser again so that it's not duplicated, but I'm not sure if / how that's possible - and it would still require some preprocessing so that it fully processes which I think still requires knowing all options that take arguments.

I'm happy to dig further in this but if you have some words of wisdom to guide me that would be appreciated.

