This has been partially solved: the `.stack` property is now preserved. That's quite useful when printing the error in a console.

However, the `.name` and `.constructor` properties are converted to a generic `Error` instead. 
More importantly, the `.message` property becomes an empty string.

```js
import {serialize, deserialize} from 'node:v8';

const error = AbortSignal.abort().reason;
console.log(error);
// DOMException [AbortError]: This operation was aborted
//   at ...
console.log(error.stack);
// AbortError: This operation was aborted
//   at ...
console.log(error.message);
// This operation was aborted
console.log(error instanceof DOMException);
// true
console.log(error.name);
// AbortError
console.log(error.constructor);
// [class DOMException]

const clonedError = deserialize(serialize(error));
console.log(clonedError);
// AbortError: This operation was aborted
//   at ...
console.log(clonedError.stack);
// AbortError: This operation was aborted
//   at ...
console.log(clonedError.message);
// Empty string
console.log(clonedError instanceof DOMException);
// false
console.log(clonedError.name);
// Error
console.log(clonedError.constructor);
// [Function: Error]
```

This is unlike how other built-in error classes (like `TypeError`) are serialized by V8.

```js
import {serialize, deserialize} from 'node:v8';

const error = new TypeError('test')
console.log(error);
// TypeError: test
//   at ...
console.log(error.stack);
// TypeError: test
//   at ...
console.log(error.message);
// test
console.log(error instanceof TypeError);
// true
console.log(error.name);
// TypeError
console.log(error.constructor);
// [Function: TypeError]

const clonedError = deserialize(serialize(error));
console.log(clonedError);
// TypeError: test
//   at ...
console.log(clonedError.stack);
// TypeError: test
//   at ...
console.log(clonedError.message);
// test
console.log(clonedError instanceof TypeError);
// true
console.log(clonedError.name);
// TypeError
console.log(clonedError.constructor);
// [Function: TypeError]
```

(As a side note, it seems like `v8.serialize()` discards properties set on error instances like `error.custom = true`, but that behavior happens with any error class, so it's out of scope of this issue)

