I would be *really* glad to see web workers in `node`. I have spent literally hundreds of hours over the last decade trying to write portable web worker code, and my experience agrees with a lot of others in this thread:

- Web workers are the standard way to do non-blocking work in JS, which is critical for some applications and libraries.
- `node` is the main environment which uses a non-standard worker API, and it's *significantly* more work to support portable worker code if you want it to work in `node`. I'm often tempted to say "it's not worth it for this project". By contrast, other non-browser environments like Deno work out of the box.
  - Even libraries dedicated to web workers, like [Comlink](https://github.com/GoogleChromeLabs/comlink), have trouble working with `node` out of the box, and require careful setup. I had to fork and modify the code so that I could avoid `.unref()` issues in `node`.
  - I have yet to see reliable wrapper for `worker_threads` that implements an API sufficiently compatible with web workers. I'm glad to see people trying, but the resulting libraries are always missing compatibility details, or outright fail to work with some environments and/or bundlers. At this point, I think it's a fair assessment that `worker_threads` is not sufficiently compatible for the lay coder.

I think it would be sufficiently useful even if just module workers were supported, as ESM's `import.meta` URL is already needed to instantiate a web worker in the de facto portable relative syntax (once Firefox finishes implementing support):

> ```js
> new Worker(new URL("./worker.js", import.meta.url).href, { type: "module" });
> ```

(It may soon become feasible to use `new Worker(import.meta.resolve("./worker.js"), { type: "module" });` instead, but the same point about ESM stands.)

For maximally portable code (i.e. a single implementation that [works for CDN-hosted libraries](https://github.com/lgarron/web-worker-compat-problems#problem-7-web-workers-cannot-be-instantiated-cross-origin), without environment sniffing), it would further be valuable if the `node` implementation was compatible with an object URL trampoline: 

```js
// The following four lines work in `node` these days!
const workerURL = new URL("./worker.js", import.meta.url);
const importSrc = `import ${JSON.stringify(workerURL)};`;
const blob = new Blob([importSrc], { type: "text/javascript" });
const objectURL = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
// This doesn't work yet:
new Worker(objectURL, { type: "module" });
```

But that's less critical, and [blank workers](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/6911) or [module blocks](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-js-module-blocks/issues/58) will hopefully make this workaround obsolete in the long term.

As a library author, I would find this *especially* valuable, because we have often have to use careful workarounds to prevent bundlers from making changes to our code that break different environments. If the same code path works in browsers and `node`, this concern would change from a fundamental risk into a (hopefully) rare edge case.

As an added benefit, it would be nice to avoid an import of `worker_threads`  (or `node:worker_threads`). Many bundlers still detect such an import as a dependency and try to install, bundle, or error — even if it's a runtime dependency that is only imported when running in `node`. It's not a great experience if someone tries to use our library, only to find that that their bundler is suddenly complaining about this thing called `worker_threads` that they've never heard of — if it's not reliable and painless for someone to use a library that depends on workers, then the end user experience/performance can suffer. (For the time being, we try to [obfuscate the import](https://github.com/cubing/cubing.js/blob/4bbcc1e19bdbb9da5d63a885dd620c1238c362a4/src/cubing/vendor/comlink-everywhere/inside/index.ts#L9) so that bundlers don't detect it as a static dependency, which in turn requires [more workarounds](https://github.com/cubing/cubing.js/blob/4bbcc1e19bdbb9da5d63a885dd620c1238c362a4/src/cubing/vendor/comlink-everywhere/inside/index.ts#L18) to avoid issues with bundlers.)

> Is it something you'd be ready to implement this yourself?

If it makes a difference, I'd be interested in pitching in, if it can be done without investing extensive time in learning the codebase.

IMHO, implementing the [Web Workers API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Using_web_workers#web_workers_api) in a strict way such as Deno has chosen to do isn't useful. The spec is very web-centric: it admits "classic" or "module" web workers (default is "classic"), which is no concern for a runtime such as Node (here, the concern would be at best cjs vs esm). This is way too verbose:

```ts
const worker = new Worker(new URL("worker.ts", import.meta.url).href, {
  type: "module"
});
```

I suggest going the Bun route and implementing a minimal version of the Web Workers API that makes it work better for server-side use cases, i.e.

```ts
const worker = new Worker("worker.ts"); // relative to `import.meta.dirname`

// add listeners with `worker.addEventlistener`
// post messages with `worker.postMessage`
```

@Pauan I'm not in disagreement with you. But the fact is that unless "classic" mode is implemented (the default, in the spec), you won't have complete support of the Worker API. And "classic" is, unfortunately, meaningless for a runtime. As said, the API is very browser-centric.

So you're not going to be able to implement the entire Worker API, anyway. Not without deciding on the meaning of "classic" in Node.

I'm suggesting the Bun route here: treat "classic" normally (as you would an `.mjs` or a `.js` file), i.e. allow imports.

