Example is from here: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/11750041/how-to-create-a-named-pipe-in-node-js


The problem is that, AFAIK, in Windows named pipes are created using the network subsystem, not using files.

To anyone reading this from the future, the correct solution is:
```javascript
server.listen({
    path: PIPE_PATH,
    readableAll: true/false,
    writableAll: true/false,
},function(){
    L('Server: on listening');
})
```
This was implemented in 531b4bedcac14044f09129ffb65dab71cc2707d9, i.e. 5 years before this issue was even posted.
It's also a duplicate of #30823 which was also closed as "wontfix" 4 years prior, and #19154, which is the original issue.

However, there IS a bug, which could justify reopening this issue. The bug is in [this line](https://github.com/theanarkh/node/blob/7f5679b901b28c9dd330a4f7391da626602452f2/lib/net.js#L1651) in `lib/net.js`. It does not set the permissions if both `readableAll` and `writableAll` are set to `false`. However, the default permissions on Windows, when no permissions are set, are `readableAll: true, writableAll: false`, which means that even if `readableAll: false` is explicitly set, no call to `fchmod` is made.

A workaround is to use
```javascript
server.listen(PIPE_PATH,function(){
    server._handle.fchmod(0);
    L('Server: on listening');
})
```
However, this is not a pretty solution, since it relies on internal implementation details that are not part of the public API.

A better solution would be if `Server.prototype.listen` had different handling for the case where `readableAll` is `false` and where it's `undefined`.

