```

Is there anything about the metadata blob thing that you think is insufficient for differentiation? We could require all core channels to include those with some marker to indicate it is from core, and I feel like that gives us more flexibility to communicate other useful things than just a lift-and-shift rename of everything. Either way will require code modification, at least with the metadata blob approach we get more utility out of it and don't break tools that assume the original name.

We could also do the remapping thing I mentioned and make that one-directional, so `core:something` would also broadcast as `not-core:something`, but not the other way around. That'd actually be useful for channel versioning, now that I think about it. One could express backwards compatibility by remapping old channel names to new channel names, but not the other way around, to express a lack of forwards compatibility.

You are suggesting?:

```js
dc.subscribe('some.event', (data, _, meta) => {
	if (meta?.core === true) {
		// do special things
		return
	}

	// do standard things
})
```

Yes, exactly. If you subscribe to a named event you will always receive it, but you can have an extra bag of metadata to do some additional things if you happen to want some conditional logic. Generally we would try to use differently named channels so you don't have branching logic with event handlers, you can just choose to care about a particular channel or not, but I think in this particular case the fact they are semantically the same makes that not make so much sense.

We could _potentially_ also change the subscribe such that you can provide an object to match metadata against, but that would get complicated, especially if we want partial matching or exclusion rules.

```js
const matches = {
	core: false
}
dc.subscribe('some.event', matches, (data) => {
	// only triggers when core === false
