> const transferredBytes = new Uint8Array(buffer.buffer.transfer());

FWIW, you likely wouldn't want to do this with `Buffer` unless you know for certain how it was created, as `Buffer` instances are often backed by a shared ArrayBuffer pool. Transferring it away would cause all Buffers derived from that shared pool to become invalidated, not just the one you think you're transferring. With `Buffer.alloc(...)`, the `Buffer` is not shared, but with `Buffer.allocUnsafe(...)` it is shared. 

The example here also does not appropriately take the byte offset and byte length into consideration.

