> My working theory is the following:
> 
> 1. https://github.com/libuv/libuv/pull/3927 _removes_ a timer phase, running all timers only after `poll`
> 2. Node.js http stack schedules some intervals and timers internally to do its job. Those were allowed to "trigger" _before_ the `poll` phase as well.
> 3. When receiving an incoming connection (not kept-alive), there are "two" I/O events (one for the incoming socket, one for the incoming data).
> 4. In libuv < 1.45.0, those two events always happened in one single poll phase, because the data was already there. This happened because of the timers firing _before_ the incoming request, "slowing" things down.
> 5. In libuv >= 1.46.0, those two events happen in two separate poll phases, because the "work" done by the timers is not done.
> 
> So, you were relying on libuv being "slower".
> 
> Note that this happens because it just need 2-4 immediate calls to "starve" the event loop - which has protection for exactly this case, but the number is just higher: 8. https://github.com/libuv/libuv/blob/49b6e4db0cfc2bdb4c4151030618981c2fc0795b/src/unix/core.c#L462-L465.  

