I really wish we didn't have to do back flips to achieve such a simple thing, but for anyone using TypeScript, here's one way to determine ~~**SAFELY**~~ safe -ish -ly:
```typescript
import { getSystemErrorMap } from "node:util";

const systemErrorMap = getSystemErrorMap();
function isSystemError(error: unknown): error is NodeJS.ErrnoException {
  if (!(error instanceof Error) || !Object.hasOwn(error, "errno")) {
    return false;
  }

  const { errno } = <typeof error & { errno: unknown }>error;

  return typeof errno === "number" && systemErrorMap.has(errno);
}
```

> How are we supposed to check then?

Wouldn't the following give you the correct information in most cases?

```typescript
import { getSystemErrorMap } from "node:util";

const systemErrorMap = getSystemErrorMap();
function isSystemError(error: unknown): error is NodeJS.ErrnoException {
  let errno: unknown;
  try {
    errno = error?.errno
  } catch {
    // In case `errno` is a getter that throws:
    return false;
  }
  return typeof errno === "number" && systemErrorMap.has(errno);
}
```

It's rather easy to imagine false positive, but there's simply no method that won't give you any. The ECMAScript spec does not give any reliable way to check what is the "actual type" of an object (with the notable exception of `Array.isArray`), TypeScript also does not bother with that and relies on duck typing – you can only go so far to check if an object is what it pretends to be.

If you feel vindicated, you could try to add a stricter strict to check if the object has an `[[ErrorData]]` internal field:

```typescript
function isError(error: unknown): error is Error {
  return error != null && typeof error === 'object' &&
         // This will have false negative if it was set manually (either on the object or somewhere on its prototype chain):
         !(Symbol.toStringTag in error) && 
         // This will have false negatives/positives if Object.prototype.toString has been mutated:
         Object.prototype.toString.call(error) === '[object Error]';
}
```

And let's face it, knowing that an object is a genuine `Error` gives you very little useful information, you'd still have to either check each property you're using with a runtime check, or assume it has the shape you expect. Since you have to make assumptions and/or runtime checks, you might as well assume the object you're getting a genuine `SystemError` object if it has a valid `errno` property.

