A few more quirks I discovered with the existing async hook behavior for the re-invented `require()` in imported CJS (it goes all the way to v20)

1.  It gets `import` in its conditions array, instead of `require` (because it's using ESM resolution)
2. The `resolve` hook gets the full URL, not the specifier
3. The `load` hook must override the nullish source for CommonJS by e.g. reading the code from disk - if not, it uses the original require instead of the re-invented one, so the async hooks won't get triggered for `require()`.

```js
import { inspect } from 'node:util';
export async function resolve(specifier, context, nextResolve) {
  fs.writeSync(1, `resolve ${specifier} with ${inspect(context)}\n`);
  return nextResolve(specifier, context);
}


export async function load(url, context, nextLoad) {
  const result = await nextLoad(url, context);
  // Without this, async hooks won't get triggered for `require()` in imported CJS
  result.source ??= fs.readFileSync(new URL(url), 'utf8');
  return result;
}
```

```js
// The resolve hook gets file://path/to/cjs.cjs as the specifier, not `./cjs.cjs`
// and context.conditions is [ 'node', 'import', 'module-sync', 'node-addons' ]
require('./cjs.cjs');
```

