Then the performance gap disappears:

<img width="647" height="210" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42503e1b-543c-4065-ae05-4d9d4813d236" />

(Don’t forget that an ESM entry point itself is slightly slower than a CommonJS entry point; changing both files to just `console.log('typescript')` with no import/require produces a result where the CommonJS entry is 1.09 times faster, a gap of about 2 ms.)

The approach in the above code is essentially a way of defining the named exports in CommonJS, without creating any new APIs. The pattern here is one that `cjs-module-lexer` recognizes, and by putting the huge file one level down and `require`-ing it, we remove the need for `cjs-module-lexer` to need to parse the big file. Even _this_ file presumably takes a millisecond or two to parse, because for whatever reason `typescript` has 2,244 exports (which is probably part of the problem in parsing the big file too). If the TypeScript team wanted to export only their documented public APIs, and use this wrapper file approach, then the performance gap between requiring and importing would become so small we’d struggle to measure it.

From adding the following additional logging, I can confirm that the specific issue is that the `drain` event is not firing (and `writableNeedDrain` is not resetting to `false`) despite the stream draining:

```js
function drainUncorked(target) {
	console.log(target.writableLength, target.writableHighWaterMark, target.writableNeedDrain);
	// existing implementation from above
}

const s = createServer(async (req, res) => {
	res.on('drain', () => console.log('got drain event'));
	const i = setInterval(() => console.log(res.writableLength, res.writableHighWaterMark, res.writableNeedDrain), 10);
	// existing handling code from above
	clearInterval(i);
}
```

> ```
> listening
> request
> 1
> 2
> 1000237 65536 true
> drain required: true
> 1000002 65536 true
> 1000002 65536 true
> 1000002 65536 true
> got drain event
> drain complete
> 3
> 4
> 1000100 65536 true
> drain required: true
> 0 65536 true           <-- note: writableLength has returned to 0 but writableNeedDrain is still true and no drain event has fired
> 0 65536 true
> [repeats infinitely]
> ```

A workaround for this issue is to use the callback of `write` instead of listening for `drain`:

```diff
- target.once('drain', () => { ... });
+ target.write('', () => { ... });
```

