The memory leak issue is eliminated with the example below which has an alternative race implementation. 

However if you uncomment the line `resolve = null` the memory leak comes back. 

Thanks to @bnoordhuis https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/29385#issuecomment-526822293 for the approach.

```js
async function promiseValue(value) {
  return value;
}

function race(promises) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const tidyResolve = (value) => {
      if (resolve) resolve(value);
      resolve = null;
    };
    promises.forEach((promise) => promise.then(tidyResolve, reject));
  });
}

async function run() {
  for (;;) {
    await race([promiseValue("foo"), promiseValue("bar")]);
  }
}

run();
```

The references are held by the [next tick queue](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/48c75bc02b24445199bc7cd3bc0cb54da369d197/lib/internal/process/promises.js#L220), and as the event loop could not start, the promises aren't gc'd. This happens because `Promise.race` [is one of the cases where v8 resolve promises multiple times](https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/41554#issuecomment-1014739641).

This does not happen in [d8 because it ignores those events](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/48c75bc02b24445199bc7cd3bc0cb54da369d197/deps/v8/src/d8/d8.cc#L3898).

An option to fix it could be calling `nextTick` only if the `multipleResolves` has been registered:

```js
function resolveError(type, promise, reason) {
  // We have to wrap this in a next tick. Otherwise the error could be caught by
  // the executed promise.
  if (process.eventNames().includes('multipleResolves')) {
    process.nextTick(() => {
      process.emit('multipleResolves', type, promise, reason);
      multipleResolvesDeprecate();
    });
  }
}
```

This is possible using undici: https://github.com/nodejs/undici/blob/main/docs/docs/api/WebSocket.md#new-websocketurl-protocol

```js
import { Agent } from 'undici'

const agent = new Agent({
  connect: {
    rejectUnauthorized: false
  }
})

new WebSocket('wss://echo.websocket.org', {
  dispatcher: agent
})
```

Also a funny thing from a "ecosystem as a whole" perspective: Bun doesn't work with WebSocket packages other than 'ws' which they polyfill (https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/20547), but they do have a 'WebSocket' constructor that accepts this:
```ts
import { Agent, WebSocket as UndiciWebSocket } from "undici";
// ...
if (process.versions.bun) {
  const client = new WebSocket(`${protocol}://localhost:${port}/custom/selftest`, {
    tls: { rejectUnauthorized: false }
  } as unknown as string);
  // ...
} else {
  const agent = new Agent({ connect: { rejectUnauthorized: false } });
  const client = new UndiciWebSocket(`${protocol}://localhost:${port}/custom/selftest`, {
    dispatcher: agent
  });
  // ...
}
```

